Tension-Type Headache
Common Infections and Afflictions
Bilateral band-like pain, Pressing non-throbbing quality, Absence of nausea

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: CIA-041
Reason for Visit: Constant, oppressive headache unresponsive to over-the-counter medication.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Patient triaged via telephone reporting a persistent, bilateral "vise-like" headache that has intensified over the past 8 hours, causing significant distress and preventing sleep. Given the presentation of new, severe head pain, an immediate on-site clinical assessment is required to perform a comprehensive neurological examination and definitively rule out secondary causes such as hypertensive crisis, temporal arteritis, or intracranial hemorrhage. Delaying this assessment poses an unacceptable risk of missing a life-threatening neurological event, strictly fulfilling the criteria for an urgent after-hours MBS assessment (MBS 585-600).

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: My head feels like it's being squeezed in a band of iron, and it just won't let up.
History of Present Illness: Symptoms initiated 12 hours ago during a period of high occupational stress and fatigue. The pain is described as a bilateral, non-throbbing, and band-like sensation. It has a pressing or tightening quality of moderate intensity. The patient notes that the pain is not aggravated by routine physical activity. Notably, there is an absolute absence of nausea and vomiting. The patient reports mild phonophobia but denies photophobia. Attacks typically last from 30 minutes to several days.
Functional Status: Independent, but currently restricted by pain and fatigue.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone. High situational stress levels noted.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 135/85 mmHg | HR: 82 | Temp: 36.6 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 78 Kg
General: Alert, well-perfused. Appears tired and mildly distressed, frequently rubbing the temples and neck.
Recent/Relevant Labs: None available.
Physical Exam: Neurological examination is entirely normal: cranial nerves II-XII intact, 5/5 strength in all extremities, normal gait, and symmetrical reflexes. Fundoscopy reveals no papilledema. Significant pericranial muscle tenderness is elicited on palpation of the temporalis and trapezius muscles. Temporal pulses are palpable and non-tender.
Environmental Observations: Workplace laptop open; evidence of prolonged sedentary screen time.
Medication Reconciliation: No regular medications. Patient has consumed 1000mg Paracetamol and 400mg Ibuprofen with negligible relief.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Tension-Type Headache - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:
Migraine without Aura - Ruled out due to the bilateral distribution, non-throbbing quality, and the definitive absence of nausea or significant photophobia.
Hypertensive Headache - Ruled out by the lack of severe blood pressure elevation and the pressing (rather than pulsatile) nature of the pain.
Temporal Arteritis - Unlikely given the bilateral nature, absence of jaw claudication, and presence of normal, non-tender temporal arteries.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY
The patient presented with a severe, constant, and oppressive headache that failed to respond to standard analgesia, necessitating an urgent evaluation to exclude secondary, life-threatening intracranial or vascular pathology. A thorough neurological and cardiovascular examination confirmed stable vitals and a completely normal focal neurological status. The identification of classic pericranial muscle tenderness and the "band-like" pain quality confirmed a primary tension-type headache. By providing immediate clinical reassurance and a targeted management plan, a highly distressing and unnecessary emergency department presentation for neuroimaging was averted. This decisive assessment satisfies MBS 585-600 criteria.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered 1000mg Paracetamol and 400mg Ibuprofen orally stat (as previous doses were >6 hours prior). Advised regular Paracetamol 1000mg every 6 hours if required.
Positive Behavioral Change: To physically release the muscle tension causing the "iron band" sensation, perform a gentle neck-stretching routine every 2 hours. Gently tilt your head toward each shoulder for 30 seconds; this stretches the trapezius muscles and breaks the feedback loop of tension that keeps the headache active.
Home-Based Services: None.
DME/Safety: None.
Follow-up: Review with regular General Practitioner in 1 week if headaches become frequent (more than twice weekly).

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN
Call 000 immediately if you experience a "thunderclap" headache (the worst pain of your life starting instantly), sudden weakness on one side of the body, difficulty speaking, or a high fever with a stiff neck.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is clinical; new or changing headaches require ongoing monitoring.
Clinical Course: Tension headaches typically resolve within 24-48 hours once the initial trigger (stress/fatigue) is addressed. Recurrence is common if lifestyle triggers are not managed.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE
Seek immediate review if the headache becomes pulsatile, is accompanied by nausea, or if you notice a change in your vision.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The muscles in your neck and scalp have essentially gone into a prolonged spasm due to stress, creating a "band" of pressure. The medications reduce inflammation, but physical relaxation is the only way to "unlock" the spasm.
Actionable Steps: Take a warm shower focusing the water on your neck and shoulders to promote blood flow. Ensure your workstation is ergonomically correct to prevent chronic neck strain.

